# [tool.uv]
# exclude-newer = "2026-10-15T00:00:00Z"
# ///

# Formatted with ruff's default settings: uvx ruff format <this file>

"""Pantheon runs a small team of AI coding agents in one terminal.

Each agent is a real CLI (claude, codex, or gemini; claude can also run on an ollama
model) with one role. A mode decides who works when. Pantheon shows the team in a
chat-room feed, gives each agent a tab with a live copy of its screen, and lets you
"transport" into an agent's own terminal when it needs you. Ctrl-] brings you back.

  pantheon                          open the session switcher
  pantheon start MODE [OPTIONS] [TASK]
                                    start a session, e.g. `pantheon start implement 123`
      -C, --dir DIR                 start in DIR (default: the current directory)
      -a, --agent NAME=SPEC         change an agent, e.g. reviewer=gemini:pro
      -y, --yolo                    start agents with permission checks off
  pantheon session SLUG             open a session (a unique part of the slug is enough)
  pantheon sessions [--prune DAYS]  list sessions; --prune deletes finished ones
  pantheon show SLUG [NAME]         print a session's room, or one of its results
  pantheon configure                detect agents again
  pantheon help [COMMAND]

SPEC is TOOL[:MODEL][@EFFORT], where TOOL is claude, codex, gemini, or ollama. TASK is
free text, an issue number (123 or #123), or an issue URL.

Agents keep your own setup: agent files, skills, MCP servers, and permission rules.
Pantheon gives each step a short brief, and the agent writes its result to a file that
ends with a "PANTHEON: <status>" line. Each session has a private tmux server, so agents
keep running when pantheon exits. Claude and codex report through hooks; gemini reports
through its window title.

Files: ~/.local/state/pantheon (or $PANTHEON_HOME) holds config.toml and one sqlite file per
session, sessions/<slug>.sqlite3. That file is the whole session: its record, its lock,
the briefs, and the results. Agents read and write plain files, so while they run,
pantheon also keeps their briefs and results in run/<slug>/, and copies each result into
the session file.
"""

